  "original": "RAMANATHAN, P. | NPS-2025-0417 | Intake Assessment 2025-01-14 | SYNTHETIC TEST RECORD\nPage 1\nNORTHBRIDGE PSYCHOLOGICAL SERVICES\nSuite 340, 10123 Whitemud Crossing NW, Edmonton, AB T6J 2R4 | T: (780) 555-0148 | F: (780) 555-0149\nCOMPREHENSIVE PSYCHOLOGICAL INTAKE ASSESSMENT\n Client:\nRAMANATHAN, Priya S.\nFile No:\nNPS-2025-0417\nDOB:\n1989-04-17 (35 yrs)\nPHN:\n6047-2231-9\nAddress:\n8842 - 112 Street NW, Edmonton, AB\nPhone:\n(587) 555-0273 (cell, may leave VM)\nReferred by:\nDr. H. Okonjo, MD, CCFP, Millwoods\nFamily Clinic\nReferral date:\n2024-11-28\nAssessment date:\nJanuary 14, 2025\nDuration:\n90 min\nClinician:\nL. Fontaine-Berg, R.Psych (#4471)\nModality:\nIn-person\nEmergency\ncontact:\nR. Ramanathan (spouse), (587) 555-0311\nConsent:\nWritten, obtained 2025-01-14\n1. REASON FOR REFERRAL\nMs. Ramanathan is a 35-year-old married female referred by her family physician for assessment and treatment of\npersistent low mood. The referral letter notes a six-year history of depressive symptoms with two prior partial medication\ntrials and describes the client as \"increasingly withdrawn, missing work approximately two days per week.\" Netcare review\nconfirms prior prescriptions and one previous course of counselling in 2021 (six sessions, private provider, records not\navailable).\n2. PRESENTING CONCERNS\nClient reports low mood present \"most days, for years, but worse since about last spring.\" She describes anhedonia\n(\"nothing lands anymore\"), early morning awakening at approximately 4:30 AM with inability to return to sleep, total sleep of\n4 to 5 hours nightly, appetite reduction with unintentional weight loss of 6 kg over four months, and marked fatigue.\nConcentration difficulties have affected her work as a payroll administrator at Halverson Logistics Inc.; she reports\nre-checking her work \"four or five times\" and still fearing errors.\nClient endorses pervasive guilt and self-criticism, describing herself as \"a drain on everyone.\" She denies current suicidal\nideation but acknowledges passive thoughts approximately one to two times per month over the past year, described as\n\"wishing I could just not wake up.\" She denies plan, intent, or preparatory behaviour. No history of self-harm. No prior\npsychiatric hospitalization. Denies homicidal ideation.\n3. HISTORY OF PRESENTING PROBLEM\nFirst depressive episode reported at age 22 during final year of undergraduate study, following the death of her father.\nEpisode lasted approximately eight months, untreated. Second episode at age 28 following a workplace restructuring;\ntreated with sertraline via family physician. Client reports partial benefit but discontinued after roughly three months due to\ngastrointestinal side effects. Third and current episode has an insidious onset dated by the client to spring 2018, with no\nclear return to premorbid functioning since. This pattern is consistent with a chronic course.\n4. RELEVANT HISTORY\nMedical: Iron deficiency anemia diagnosed 2016, treated intermittently. Migraine, approximately two episodes per month.\nNo head injury, seizures, or thyroid disease documented. Last physical examination 2024-10-09.\nMedication: Escitalopram 10 mg daily, initiated 2024-12-05 by Dr. Okonjo. Client reports adherence \"most days.\"\nSumatriptan 50 mg PRN. No known drug allergies.\nSubstance use: Alcohol 2 to 4 standard drinks weekly, no escalation reported. Denies cannabis or other substance use.\nCaffeine 3 cups daily.\nFamily: Mother with documented depression, treated. Paternal uncle described by client as having \"problems with his\nnerves,\" details unknown. No known family history of bipolar disorder.\nSocial: Married nine years, no children. Spouse employed as a millwright, described as supportive but \"tired of it.\" Two\nclose friends, contact reduced over the past year. No current recreational activities; formerly ran three times weekly until\n2022.\n\n\nRAMANATHAN, P. | NPS-2025-0417 | Intake Assessment 2025-01-14 | SYNTHETIC TEST RECORD\nPage 2\n5. MENTAL STATUS EXAMINATION\nAppearance neat, appropriately dressed for weather. Psychomotor retardation evident; latency of response averaged three\nto four seconds. Speech reduced in rate and volume. Mood self-described as \"flat, mostly.\" Affect restricted, congruent.\nThought process linear, mildly impoverished. No delusional content, no perceptual disturbance elicited. Cognition grossly\nintact; oriented x3. Insight fair, judgment intact. Rapport established slowly but adequately by the second half of the session.\n6. PSYCHOMETRICS ADMINISTERED\nInstrument\nRaw score\nRange / Interpretation\nNotes\nPHQ-9\n22\n20 to 27 = severe\nItem 9 endorsed at 1 (several days)\nGAD-7\n14\n10 to 14 = moderate\nBDI-II\n34\n29 to 63 = severe\nCognitive subscale elevated\nWSAS\n27\n20 to 40 = moderately severe impairment\nWork and social leisure most affected\nC-SSRS (screen)\nPositive, item 2\nNon-specific active thoughts, no plan/intent\nLow acute risk at present\nAUDIT-C\n3\nBelow cut-off\n7. DIAGNOSTIC IMPRESSION\n296.33 (F33.2) Major Depressive Disorder, Recurrent episode, Severe, without psychotic features, with anxious distress.\n300.4 (F34.1) Persistent Depressive Disorder, with intermittent major depressive episodes, with current episode. Early\nonset.\nRule out: contribution of iron deficiency to fatigue presentation. Bloodwork recommended.\n8. CASE FORMULATION\nPredisposing factors include family history of mood disorder and early paternal bereavement. Precipitating factors include\nworkplace restructuring in 2018 and progressive social withdrawal. Perpetuating factors are the central treatment target: a\nwell-established cycle of behavioural withdrawal reducing opportunity for reward, negative automatic thoughts centred on\nthemes of inadequacy and burdensomeness, and rumination which the client experiences as problem-solving. Protective\nfactors include an intact marriage, stable employment to date, absence of substance misuse, and prior help-seeking.\n9. RECOMMENDATIONS AND TREATMENT PLAN\na. Individual Cognitive Behavioural Therapy, weekly, initial course of 12 sessions with review at session 6.\nb. Behavioural activation to be introduced from session 2 given the degree of withdrawal.\nc. Continue escitalopram; recommend family physician review dose at four weeks.\nd. Bloodwork requested: CBC, ferritin, B12, TSH, given fatigue and anemia history.\ne. Outcome monitoring: PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at every third session.\nf. Sleep hygiene psychoeducation, session 3.\ng. Risk to be re-screened at minimum every fourth session or on clinical indication.\nSigned: L. Fontaine-Berg, R.Psych, Registration #4471   |   Date signed: 2025-01-16   |   Next appointment: 2025-01-28, 10:00\nThis record contains health information as defined under the Alberta Health Information Act. Unauthorized access, use, or disclosure is prohibited.\nSynthetic record, not a real patient.",
